      class: "CD3-directed T-cell-modulating disease-modifying type-1-diabetes monoclonal antibody",
      usedToTreat: "Delay onset of stage 3 type 1 diabetes in adults and children age 8 years and older with stage 2 disease; current labeling also includes delaying beta-cell-function decline in children age 8 to 17 years with recently diagnosed stage 3 type 1 diabetes using the product-specific course schedule.",
      description: "Teplizumab-mzwv is an IV anti-CD3 monoclonal antibody that modifies autoreactive T-cell behavior rather than replacing insulin. In stage 2 type 1 diabetes it delays progression to symptomatic stage 3 disease; current 2026 labeling also covers preservation of beta-cell function in selected children with newly diagnosed stage 3 disease. It does not cure autoimmunity or guarantee that insulin will never be needed.",
      mechanism: "Teplizumab binds CD3 on CD4 and CD8 T lymphocytes, partially signals through the T-cell receptor complex and internalizes the CD3-antibody complex. The resulting program can deactivate pancreatic-beta-cell-autoreactive T cells, increase regulatory T-cell proportion and increase exhausted CD8 phenotypes. This changes immune pressure on beta cells while transiently causing lymphopenia and cytokine release.",
